+# Decision: How committed changes reach browsers
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit an event revision. Something then has to carry those revisions to every browser showing that event: up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers across forty markets, with a busy market averaging two status changes per minute and opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second.
+
+The board replaces group chats that coordinators use to relay stall closures, late arrivals, and power problems. A board that lags the chat it replaces gives coordinators a reason to keep the chat, so how quickly a change appears matters beyond the stated freshness goal.
+
+The delivery mechanism is not a detail that can be swapped later at low cost. It determines what the change store must keep and publish, what the delivery component runs, and how browsers subscribe and recover after a drop. Those three components are designed around whichever mechanism is chosen, so a wrong choice is paid for in all three.
+
+Every option considered meets the project's goals: changes visible within 15 seconds, recovery after a 20-minute disconnection, and seven days of retained revisions. Every option also respects the tenets that a committed edit has one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route and that connectivity failures must be visible. Those are background to this decision, not what it turns on.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+There are no gates. The tenet *self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* means depending on a provider does not eliminate an option; it is weighed below.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Update latency — major.** How soon after commit a change appears in a connected browser, in the normal case. The goal sets a 15-second outer bound; this criterion is about how far inside it the board normally sits.
+- **Provider independence — major.** Whether the board's delivery path, credentials, and browser subscription model are bound to one external realtime provider, and what it would take to leave.
+- **Recurring cost — moderate.** Ongoing fees that scale with connections or messages, beyond the infrastructure the board already runs.
+- **Operational burden — moderate.** What the team must run, size, monitor, and debug itself, including during the opening-time burst and within the 99.5 percent availability goal.
+- **Build effort — minor.** How much has to be designed and built across the change store, delivery, and browser sync before the first event can use it.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Each browser asks the server every five seconds for revisions after the last one it has seen. The change store answers cursor queries ("revisions for this event after N"); delivery is an ordinary stateless request/response endpoint; the browser runs a timer loop. There are no long-lived connections, so there is no connection state to manage, and a browser returning after a drop simply resumes asking from its cursor. Recovery and normal operation are the same code path. It scales and fails like the rest of an HTTPS service.
+
+- Update latency: **weak**. Every change waits up to five seconds for the next poll; subsecond updates are not possible without shortening the interval and multiplying read load.
+- Provider independence: **strong**. No external realtime dependency.
+- Recurring cost: **partial**. No provider fee, but 800 browsers at a five-second interval produce about 160 reads per second whether or not anything changed.
+- Operational burden: **strong**. Stateless, with no connection capacity or reconnect behaviour to operate.
+- Build effort: **strong**. The least to design in all three components.
+
+Summary: trades latency for the simplest operation.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+Each browser holds a WebSocket connection to servers MarketBoard runs and subscribes to an event; the server pushes each committed revision as it is published. The change store keeps revisions replayable from a given revision and reliably publishes each one to delivery. Delivery manages socket sessions, subscriptions, and capacity for 800 connections. The browser reconnects after a drop and catches up from its last applied revision. Commands remain on HTTPS.
+
+- Update latency: **strong**. Changes are pushed on commit, with no polling interval.
+- Provider independence: **strong**. The whole delivery path is the project's own.
+- Recurring cost: **strong**. No provider fee and no idle polling reads; the cost is the capacity to hold the connections.
+- Operational burden: **weak**. The team owns connection capacity, session lifecycle, behaviour during bursts and deploys, and every reconnect and catch-up failure mode.
+- Build effort: **weak**. Replay, reliable publication, session management, and reconnect logic all have to be designed and built.
+
+Summary: trades operational and build work for fast updates with no external dependency.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A realtime provider holds the browser connections. The server publishes each committed revision to the provider, which fans it out; browsers use the provider's client library and subscription model. The change store reconciles publication so that a revision committed but not delivered to the provider is detected and republished. Delivery is a thin wrapper around the provider's API and credentials. Connection scaling, reconnection, and much of the catch-up behaviour are the provider's responsibility and are already built.
+
+- Update latency: **strong**. Push delivery, comparable to self-hosted sockets.
+- Provider independence: **weak**. Delivery, subscription credentials, and browser subscription code are written against one provider; leaving means rewriting delivery and browser sync. Event updates carry only stall IDs and operational statuses, so the exposure is coupling and availability, not data sensitivity.
+- Recurring cost: **weak**. Ongoing fees that grow with connections and messages.
+- Operational burden: **strong**. Connection operations are the provider's; the team operates publication reconciliation only.
+- Build effort: **strong**. The quickest route to low-latency updates.
+
+Summary: trades recurring fees and provider coupling for low latency with little connection work.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets.
+
+It is the only option that is strong on both major criteria. Polling keeps the board independent but leaves every update seconds behind; a hosted service is fast but binds delivery and browser sync to a provider and adds a recurring fee. Self-hosted sockets give fast updates and independence together, and are also the strongest on recurring cost.
+
+The price is paid on operational burden and build effort, where this option is the weakest of the three. That is accepted deliberately: the extra connection and reconnect work is judged worth fast updates that do not tie the board to a realtime vendor. The tenet permitting managed infrastructure is not being overridden; a hosted service was allowed and was outweighed here, and the tenet continues to apply to other infrastructure choices.
+
+Committing to this obligates, downstream:
+
+- The change store keeps each event's revisions ordered and replayable from any retained revision, and publishes every committed revision to delivery reliably.
+- Delivery owns socket sessions, per-event subscriptions, and capacity for 800 simultaneous connections, including through opening-time bursts.
+- Browser sync owns reconnect, catch-up from its last applied revision, and showing when it is not connected, as the tenet on visible connectivity failures requires.
+- The 99.5 percent availability goal now covers a stateful connection tier the team runs itself.
+
+It forecloses the stateless simplicity of polling and the option of handing connection problems to a provider. The judgment is worth re-running if connection operations consume more effort than the board's scale justifies, or if the number of simultaneous browsers grows well beyond 800.
