 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets deliver committed changes.** MarketBoard runs its own socket connections and pushes each committed revision to subscribed browsers. This gives low-latency updates with no recurring realtime-provider fee and no provider coupling; the cost is owning connection capacity, session management, and reconnect recovery. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it is the simplest to operate and meets the 15-second freshness goal, but every browser re-reads on a timer and every update lags by seconds. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it is the quickest route to low latency with the least connection operations, but adds recurring fees and couples the board's delivery, credentials, and browser subscription model to one provider. The criteria and the full comparison are in `docs/decisions/change-delivery.md`.
